---
import GuideLayout from '@layouts/GuideLayout.astro';
import ReferenceBlock from '@components/ReferenceBlock.astro';
import { config } from './config';
import { withBase } from '@lib/paths';
import { categories } from '@data/categories';
import type { EcosystemEntry } from '@data/types';

interface Props {
  title: string;
  description: string;
  readMinutes: number;
  updatedAt: string;
  entry: EcosystemEntry;
}

const props = Astro.props;
const category = categories.find(c => c.slug === config.categorySlug)!;
const toolHref = withBase(`/tool/${category.segment}/${config.slug}/`);
---

<GuideLayout
  {...props}
  toolSlug={config.slug}
  toolName={config.name}
  toolCategorySlug={config.categorySlug}
>
  <section id="quick-answer">
    <h2>Quick Answer <span class="gold-dot" aria-hidden="true"></span></h2>
    <p>
      A virtual fidget spinner is a disc you flick in the browser. This one reads pointer velocity,
      not drag distance, so a short fast trackpad flick still spins. It coasts, then stops. Optional
      ticks fire when an arm crosses the top.
    </p>
    <p>
      For example: Input: a flick on a phone-width tab. Output: rotation that decays to Stopped,
      with no leftover crawl that would drain a battery in a background tab.
    </p>
    <p>
      Therefore this is a fidget, not a three-second ad. It is free, needs no download, and uploads
      nothing.
    </p>
    <a href={toolHref} class="cta-link">Open Fidget Spinner →</a>
  </section>

  <section id="what-is">
    <h2>What is a virtual fidget spinner? <span class="gold-dot" aria-hidden="true"></span></h2>
    <p>
      A physical spinner is a bearing and three arms. A browser spinner is the same gesture as
      rotation. Novelty pages often feel floaty, stop abruptly, or never stop. Ads sit around a
      toy that lasts three seconds.
    </p>
    <p>
      This disc has three arms and a hub. Flick anywhere on the stage. Momentum comes from how
      fast the pointer moved, which is why a trackpad flick that barely travels can still work.
      Other toys listened only to drag distance, which is the recorded failure.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section id="how-it-works">
    <h2>How does the spinner get momentum? <span class="gold-dot" aria-hidden="true"></span></h2>
    <p>
      Pointer movement around the centre becomes angular velocity. The Feel engine clamps a spike
      so a trackpad jump cannot run forever, then applies friction each frame. When speed falls
      below rest, the loop ends and the status reads Stopped.
    </p>
    <p>
      Hiding the tab also stops the loop. A spinner that crawls at 0.01 radians in a background
      tab is a battery leak. That rest threshold is the craft of this tool.
    </p>
    <ReferenceBlock type="key-idea" heading="Velocity, not distance">
      <p>
        A long slow drag is a weak flick. A short fast one is a strong flick. Measuring only how
        far the pointer travelled is why some web spinners feel dead on a trackpad.
      </p>
    </ReferenceBlock>
    <ReferenceBlock type="common-mistake" heading="Leaving a crawl running">
      <p>
        If the disc never quite stops, the animation loop keeps waking the phone. Stopped means
        the loop is gone, not that a tiny omega is still scheduled.
      </p>
    </ReferenceBlock>
  </section>

  <section id="ticks">
    <h2>How do tick sounds stay in sync? <span class="gold-dot" aria-hidden="true"></span></h2>
    <p>
      Ticks fire when an arm crosses 12 o'clock, from the angle, not from a timer. A long spin
      cannot desync the way a setInterval tick can. Mute under the board or Settings → Feel. Muting
      does not change haptics.
    </p>
    <p>
      Reduced motion turns rotation off. A tap still counts, so the control remains a fidget. The
      disc does not pretend to spin.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section id="common-mistakes">
    <h2>Common mistakes <span class="gold-dot" aria-hidden="true"></span></h2>
    <p>
      The first mistake is judging a flick by how far the pointer travelled. Therefore a long slow
      drag feels strong and then does nothing. For example, a two-centimetre trackpad flick that
      lasts 80 milliseconds will out-spin a slow drag across the whole disc.
    </p>
    <p>
      The second is leaving a crawl running in a background tab. However small the leftover speed
      looks, the animation loop is still scheduled. Stopped means the loop is gone.
    </p>
    <ReferenceBlock type="common-mistake" heading="Expecting reduced motion to freeze the control">
      <p>
        Reduced motion stops rotation. A tap still counts. The disc remains a fidget, not a dead
        picture.
      </p>
    </ReferenceBlock>
  </section>

  <section id="vs">
    <h2>Spinner vs alternatives <span class="gold-dot" aria-hidden="true"></span></h2>
    <p>
      A phone spinner app wants install permissions and often wraps the disc in ads. A novelty web
      toy often never stops, or stops so hard it feels broken. This page is the middle: a disc in
      the tab, velocity-based flicks, and a real rest.
    </p>
    <p>
      Compared with Gears, there is no ratio to read. Compared with Pop It, there is no board to
      clear. Pick the spinner when the gesture you want is a flick, not a tap or a mesh.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section id="try">
    <h2>Try the disc <span class="gold-dot" aria-hidden="true"></span></h2>
    <p>
      Open the tool, flick once, wait until the status reads Stopped, then try Play if you want the
      disc to fill the screen. Exit or Escape brings the page back.
    </p>
    <a href={toolHref} class="cta-link">Open Fidget Spinner →</a>
  </section>
</GuideLayout>

<style>
  .cta-link {
    display: inline-flex;
    align-items: center;
    height: var(--touch-target);
    padding-inline: var(--space-5);
    margin-top: var(--space-5);
    background: var(--color-accent);
    color: var(--color-text-inverse);
    font-weight: var(--font-weight-semibold);
    font-size: var(--text-base);
    border-radius: var(--radius-md);
    text-decoration: none;
    transition: var(--transition-color);
  }
  .cta-link:hover { opacity: 0.9; text-decoration: none; }
</style>
